  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "A medical research center is conducting experiments to examine the relationship between cigarette smoking and cancer in a particular city. The probability that a randomly selected person is a smoker that develops cancer is $0.4,$ while the probability that it is a nonsmoker that develops cancer is $0.25.$ What is the probability that a randomly selected individual from the city develops cancer?",
  "worked_solution": "Let $C$ be the event that an individual develops cancer and let $A$ be the event that individual smokes. Then ${A}^{\u2032}$ is the event that an individual does not smoke.\n\nTo calculate the probability that the randomly selected individual develops the cancer, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(C)=P(C\\cap A)+P(C\\cap {A}^{\u2032})\n$$\n\nWe  have \n\n$$\nP(C\\cap A)=0.4,\\,P(C\\cap {A}^{\u2032})=0.25\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected  individual  from the city develops cancer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C) & =P(C\\cap A)+P(C\\cap {A}^{\u2032}) \\\\  & =0.4+0.25 \\\\  & =0.65.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
